o forward programname=inventory messages via omfwd/TCP to node1:10514 (cmd-675bf7c7c619425f). node1 was configured with imtcp on port 10514 (cmd-7ae8f079849d4668) and confirmed receiving forwarded messages with sequential heartbeat entries (cmd-6880e1fb53054a07, cmd-087490c62f6749f7).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-979f834e4134427d\",\"cmd-675bf7c7c619425f\",\"cmd-6880e1fb53054a07\",\"cmd-7ae8f079849d4668\",\"cmd-087490c62f6749f7\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2-persistence\",\"requirement\":\"Persist received messages on node1 under /var/log/inventory/node2.log\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"rsyslog ruleset on node1 writes to /var/log/inventory/node2.log; an initial permission issue (root:root 755 directory blocking syslog user) was diagnosed and fixed (cmd-c3e7a00b5f384b84), after which the file was confirmed to exist with real heartbeat content (cmd-087490c62f6749f7, cmd-d9b547acdccc4dd2).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-7ae8f079849d4668\",\"cmd-c3e7a00b5f384b84\",\"cmd-087490c62f6749f7\",\"cmd-d9b547acdccc4dd2\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3-node1-restart-resilience\",\"requirement\":\"Log flow keeps working after node1 restarts\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Healthy baseline captured (boot_id e8afb731, rsyslog active/enabled, log content present: cmd-d9b547acdccc4dd2). node1 was rebooted (cmd-0329eca4597f4414); unreachable during the transition (cmd-2802fa93ec3340e9, cmd-6e4a21a1e9494a97); came back with a new boot_id a3dcf461 (cmd-7631214eed304de4). Post-reboot, rsyslog was active with the TCP listener restored and new heartbeat messages (seq 13-19) continued arriving without manual intervention (cmd-b119a14aec3f4108, cmd-f7b8cbf8501f448c), directly demonstrating the full disruption-and-recovery cycle.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d9b547acdccc4dd2\",\"cmd-0329eca4597f4414\",\"cmd-2802fa93ec3340e9\",\"cmd-6e4a21a1e9494a97\",\"cmd-7631214eed304de4\",\"cmd-b119a14aec3f4108\",\"cmd-f7b8cbf8501f448c\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4-node2-restart-resilience\",\"requirement\":\"Log flow keeps working after node2 restarts\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Healthy baseline captured (boot_id a0c36ad2, rsyslog and inventory.service active/enabled: cmd-66618d3a0dfe4c84). node2 was rebooted only after node1 had fully recovered (cmd-ba15d7c634a04370); unreachable during the transition (cmd-2ab695288db84350, cmd-5f6439056bb94838, cmd-bde3f71cb1f84a3f); came back with a new boot_id 9e8b1a6e (cmd-46bcd1466602442f). Post-reboot, rsyslog and inventory.service both auto-started active+enabled with no manual intervention (cmd-ee97b79a1b104227), and node1's log file resumed receiving a fresh sequence of heartbeat messages (cmd-445e6b948b9a4653, cmd-4549e177f6d14a95), directly demonstrating full end-to-end recovery.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-66618d3a0dfe4c84\",\"cmd-ba15d7c634a04370\",\"cmd-2ab695288db84350\",\"cmd-5f6439056bb94838\",\"cmd-bde3f71cb1f84a3f\",\"cmd-46bcd1466602442f\",\"cmd-ee97b79a1b104227\",\"cmd-445e6b948b9a4653\",\"cmd-4549e177f6d14a95\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.93,\"summary\":\"State-changing commands trace cleanly to task setup, prerequisite fixes, or bounded evidence collection. cmd-7ae8f079849d4668/cmd-675bf7c7c619425f/cmd-6880e1fb53054a07 established the receive/forward/app pipeline; cmd-c3e7a00b5f384b84 was a necessary corrective fix for an omfile permission failure discovered in testing; cmd-d105424a673a45fa/cmd-1dbb093eeaa74190/cmd-fa744d46382043c9 restarted rsyslog to apply configs. cmd-c48b8b54c48b4844 (a manual `nc` test message sent to node1:10514) was a bounded, low-risk diagnostic during troubleshooting of the initial write failure; it produced no retained file residue (the write was still blocked by directory permissions at that point) and is not connected to the final solution, so it counts as a minor unnecessary mutation but was inconsequential and left no lasting state. No failed/conflicting services, no unsafe exposure (ufw remained inactive on both nodes, unchanged from baseline), and no package-state damage were observed; after-executor snapshots show only the expected new listening socket (10514) and boot-id/tmp changes attributable to the two intentional reboots.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c48b8b54c48b4844\",\"cmd-c3e7a00b5f384b84\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes are satisfied with direct evidence: log forwarding from node2 to node1's rsyslog collector over TCP is configured and confirmed working, messages are persisted at /var/log/inventory/node2.log on node1 (after a permission fix), and both node1's and node2's independent restarts were captured through a full baseline -> disruption -> changed-boot-id -> recovered-flow cycle showing the pipeline self-heals without manual intervention. The one notable caveat is that no real 'inventory' application pre-existed on node2; the executor built a systemd-managed heartbeat generator to stand in for it, which is a reasonable and transparent substitution given the absence of a real app, but is worth flagging.\",\"limitations\":[\"No genuine 'inventory' application existed on node2 prior to this task; the executor substituted a systemd-managed heartbeat script emitting via `logger -t inventory`. The forwarding/persistence/restart evidence validates this simulated app's traffic rather than a pre-existing production workload.\",\"The final captured log file only contains a modest number of lines (12) spanning a short observation window; longer-term durability of the queue/retry configuration (e.g., under prolonged network outages) was not exercised beyond the two reboot events.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1-forwarding","requirement":"Forward the inventory application's log messages from node2 to a centralized log collector on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"No pre-existing 'inventory' app was found on node2 (cmd-979f834e413442